(i) **Characteristics of fungi**

- They are **eukaryotic** organisms (cells with a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles).
- They have **no chlorophyll**, so they cannot photosynthesise: they are **heterotrophs**, feeding as **saprophytes** (on dead organic matter) or as **parasites** (on living hosts).
- The body is made of thread-like filaments called **hyphae**, which together form a **mycelium** (yeast is the exception: it is unicellular).
- The **cell wall** is made of **chitin** (not cellulose).
- They feed by **extracellular digestion**: they secrete enzymes onto the food and **absorb** the soluble products.
- They store food as **glycogen** and oil (never as starch).
- They reproduce by **spores** (asexually, and also sexually); the spores are produced in sporangia or on fruiting bodies and are dispersed by wind.
- They have no roots, stems or leaves, and they are **non-motile**.

(ii) **Large labelled diagram of *Rhizopus* (bread mould)**

Draw a large, clear diagram with a sharp pencil, a title ("*Rhizopus* growing on bread") and label lines that do not cross. It should show:

- the **substrate** (a piece of bread) at the bottom;
- **rhizoids** (short branching root-like hyphae) growing down into the bread, to anchor the mould and absorb food;
- **stolons** (horizontal hyphae running over the surface of the bread), joining the groups of rhizoids;
- the **mycelium** (the whole network of hyphae);
- erect **sporangiophores** (upright hyphae) rising from the stolons above the rhizoids;
- at the tip of each sporangiophore a round **sporangium** (spore case) containing many **spores** (black at maturity);
- the **columella** (the dome-shaped swelling at the base inside the sporangium).
